// cyp450.mjs — the Cytochrome P450 Hub shelf: the drug-metabolism spine of the hemp-science section.
//
// WHAT THIS SHELF IS. A mechanism-first reference for the enzymes and transporters that decide how
// much of a cannabinoid, a kavalactone, a terpene or a prescription drug actually reaches the
// bloodstream and how long it stays there. Ten pages: an orientation page, six CYP isoform pages
// (3A4, 2C9, 2C19, 2D6, 1A2, 2E1), two phase-2 conjugation pages (UGT glucuronidation, SULT
// sulfonation), a transporter page (P-glycoprotein / ABCB1), and an explainer for the live
// interaction checker. Every enzyme page carries four things a pairs list cannot: what the enzyme
// metabolises, what inhibits or induces it as a DRUG, what inhibits or induces it as a BOTANICAL or
// food, and what actually happens to a patient when it is perturbed.
//
// WHERE THE MATERIAL COMES FROM. Primary literature (Bailey and Paine on grapefruit furanocoumarins;
// Volak and Bhardwaj on curcuminoids and piperine; Gurley on in-vivo botanical probe phenotyping;
// Moore and Piscitelli and Ruschitzka on St John's wort PXR induction; the Yamaori series and
// Geffrey/Morrison on cannabidiol), the FDA substrate/inhibitor/inducer table and drug-interaction
// guidance, and the Flockhart table. The botanical CYP claims the operator supplied in the Kava
// Potentiators Datasheet (Van Kush Family Research Institute, January 2026, reconstructed from the
// 2020 KavaForums potentiators thread) are represented here with their evidence grade marked —
// in-vitro potency and in-vivo human probe data are labelled as the different things they are, and
// where they disagree (kavalactones) the disagreement is stated rather than resolved.
//
// The citation records in CITES that also appear in integrations/interactions-data.mjs are reused
// VERBATIM from that file, where each DOI was resolved against the Crossref REST API on 2026-09-09.
// Records added here that were not in that set carry verified: false, which means the bibliographic
// details are recorded from the literature but no identifier has been resolved in this pass. That is
// deliberate: an unresolved citation is honest, a guessed DOI is a defect.
//
// WHAT THIS SHELF DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CONTAIN. No preparative or synthetic procedures of any kind —
// no reagents, solvents, catalysts, equivalents, temperatures, reaction times, work-ups or yields for
// making any cannabinoid or any other compound, no precursor sourcing, and no step-by-step for
// isomerisation, homologation or acetylation. Where the literature on a metabolic pathway sits next
// to a preparative literature (the allylbenzene material on the SULT page is the clearest case), only
// the metabolic fate is described and the preparative work is referred to as existing, never
// reproduced. This shelf also carries no dosing recommendations, no individualised medical advice and
// no potentiation protocols: it describes documented mechanism, including the mechanism by which a
// deliberate potentiation becomes an accidental overdose.

const F = Object.freeze;

export const SHELF = F({
  id: 'cyp450',
  title: 'Cytochrome P450 Hub',
  blurb: 'The enzymes and transporters that set cannabinoid and drug exposure: six CYP isoforms, phase-2 glucuronidation and sulfonation, P-glycoprotein efflux, and the mechanism rules that generalise where an interaction pairs list cannot.',
  updated: '2026-09-27',
});

